{{- /*
Meson options for the libvirt build. Included by images/libvirt/werf.inc.yaml.

libvirt enumerates every warning gcc knows and, for builds from a git checkout,
adds -Werror on its own:

  if (git_werror.enabled() or git_werror.auto()) and git and not get_option('werror')
    cc_flags += [ '-Werror' ]

so meson's built-in -Dwerror=false does not disable it — it is one of the
conditions that turn it on. git_werror is the option that does.

This matters now because glib2 headers come from pm instead of our own source
build: gcc declines to inline g_autoptr_cleanup_generic_gfree, and -Winline —
which only libvirt's exhaustive warning list enables — fails the build on a
header we do not own, inside libvirt's own test files. We build a pinned
release rather than develop libvirt, so warnings must not be fatal here.

The layout was implicit while we built on ALT, whose meson defaults to
prefix=/usr, libdir=lib64 and sysconfdir=/etc. This builder uses meson's own
defaults (prefix=/usr/local, libdir=lib), which would move every installed
file and break both the cleanup in werf.inc.yaml and virt-launcher, which
expects the libraries in /usr/lib64 and the configs in /etc/libvirt. Pin them,
the way container-factory pins them for the same reason.

nls is off because container-factory's gettext ships a msgfmt that cannot
start — it links a libgettextsrc.so the package does not contain — and the
package arrives transitively, so its absence cannot be relied on. Note that
this option alone is not enough: po/ is entered unconditionally and meson's
i18n module finds msgfmt on its own, so the build dependencies must also not
drag gettext in. container-factory builds libvirt without the GNU gettext
tools too, so translations were not built there either.

json_c is pinned because virJSON is how libvirt speaks QMP to qemu: built
without json-c, src/util/virjson.c compiles to stubs that report "No JSON
parser implementation is available". driver_qemu=enabled below already turns
a missing json-c into a configure error, but naming the dependency directly
points the error at the package that went away instead of at the driver.

Every other option libvirt leaves on auto is pinned to the state this build
already produces, read off its own configuration summary, so the feature set
stops depending on what pm happens to drag into the builder.

attr and libnl are pinned disabled, not enabled: neither libattr nor
libnl-devel is in the builder, and meson turns enabled-without-dependency
into a hard error. container-factory's libvirt package builds without both
as well, so this is the state the module has shipped since the move to pm.
Without libnl libvirt loses its netlink paths — SR-IOV VF address
save/restore, macvtap, its own bridges and IP setup, 802.1Qbg/bh — none of
which this module uses, because virt-launcher hands libvirt a ready tap
device. Enabling them means adding the packages first, as a separate
decision.
*/ -}}
